/K [1]
>>
endobj
133 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (Weak passwords built from personal information remain one of the most common causes of account compromise. This project implements a CUPP-style \(Common User Passwords Profiler\) custom wordlist generator in pure Python. The tool accepts a personal profile \(names, nicknames, birth dates, partner details, pet name, company and free-form keywords\), extracts base tokens, and then applies realistic human-style mangling rules: case variation, leetspeak substitution, numeric and date suffixes, special-character suffixes, and limited two-token combinations. The resulting candidate list is written to a plain-text file with a configurable safety cap \(default 200 000 words\).)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
134 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (A companion module creates self-generated sample MD5 and SHA-1 hashes from known weak passwords derived from the same profile and then runs an offline dictionary attack against those hashes. All testing is performed entirely on the local machine with invented data; no external systems or real credentials are targeted. The project demonstrates the practical effectiveness of profile-driven wordlists and the necessity of strong, unique passwords that do not reuse personal facts. It aligns with the password-auditing and dictionary-attack concepts covered in the RTU Cyber Security Lab syllabus.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
135 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 86 0 R
/T (Keywords: custom wordlist, password mangling, CUPP, leetspeak, dictionary attack, profile-based generation, MD5, SHA-1, ethical password testing.)
/E (Keywords: custom wordlist, password mangling, CUPP, leetspeak, dictionary attack, profile-based generation, MD5, SHA-1, ethical password testing.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
